  "preamble": "Monophthongization of the rising diphthong ue [wɛ] to the open-mid front rounded vowel [œ]: the rounding of the first element and the front, open-mid quality of the second fuse into one vowel. In French, Latin stressed ŏ in open syllables became uo and then ue in Old French; in Late Old French (about 1250–1300) [ue̯], together with [eu̯], became /œ/, spelled eu or œu. Modern French has [œ] in closed syllables and [ø] mostly in open ones.",
